In <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ios-27/">iOS 27</a> beta 2, Apple updated the Home app to add support for remotely updating an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/ios-27-apple-tv-home-app.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1053318" /><br/>
The &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; is now listed in the Updates section of the Home app's Settings interface, and tapping on the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; update button installs the latest software without needing to turn on the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod/">HomePod</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod-mini/">HomePod mini</a> have long been able to be updated through the Home app, so the &zwnj;iOS 27&zwnj; change for the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; puts it on par with the &zwnj;HomePod&zwnj;. The &zwnj;HomePod&zwnj; and &zwnj;HomePod mini&zwnj; run a variant of tvOS, so it makes sense for the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; to be updated in the same way.<br/>
<br/>
Apple's decision to add the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; to the Home app update interface suggests that the upcoming home hub device that's in the works will also be controlled and updated through the Home app.<br/>
<br/>
